#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Decode into the panel's own buffer instead of a heap copy.

The main heap cannot hold a 160x160 RGB565 frame and libjpeg's working pools
at the same time: 88 KB free, and taking the 51,200-byte frame first only
moved the failure into libjpeg ("Insufficient memory (case 4)").

The panel already owns a buffer of exactly the right size and format, in
PSRAM where 5.7 MB is free, and the frame was going to be copied there
anyway.  Writing each decoded row straight into it removes the largest heap
allocation entirely, removes the copy, and lets jpeg_test_display and
jpeg_test_panel_size go: the frame buffer open now supplies the geometry that
panel_size used to fetch separately.

Rows are placed at the panel's stride and centred, so the verdict walks the
image with a stride instead of assuming it is contiguous.
"""

import sys

