"""Decode into the panel's own buffer instead of a heap copy.

The main heap cannot hold a 160x160 RGB565 frame and libjpeg's working pools
at once: 88 KB free, 69 KB of it contiguous.  Taking the 51,200-byte frame
first only moved the failure into libjpeg ("Insufficient memory (case 4)"),
because the total was never going to fit.

The panel already owns a buffer of exactly the right size and format, in
PSRAM where 5.7 MB is free, and the frame was going to be copied into it
anyway.  Writing each decoded row straight there removes the largest heap
allocation, removes the copy, and lets jpeg_test_display and
jpeg_test_panel_size retire: opening the frame buffer now supplies the
geometry panel_size used to fetch on its own.

